    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a cluster of claims regarding atypical dermatological presentations. Specifically, we're seeing several cases of irregular, pigmented skin lesions that don't clearly fit into the standard L-series codes for common dermatitis or eczema. Can you walk me through the hierarchy for selecting the most specific ICD-10 code when the morphology is irregular or the presentation is non-standard? I want to make sure we aren't getting flagged for lack of specificity.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When standard L-series codes don't fit the irregular presentation, first look towards the codes for other dermatological manifestations, such as those describing pigmented lesions or cutaneous neoplasia if malignancy is suspected. If the morphology is truly atypical and no other specific code applies, consider using a general code like \"Other specified skin disorders\" (e.g., L86.9), but always document the atypical features extensively in the clinical notes to support the chosen code. Aim for the most descriptive code available within the Chapter XVI (Diseases of the skin and subcutaneous tissue) to maximize specificity.",
      "source": "target",
